      "What an Investment Company Is",
      "An investment company collects money from many investors. It uses that money to buy a portfolio of stocks and bonds. A portfolio is a collection of investments. The company then sells shares or units of that portfolio. Because each investor owns a slice of a big pool, investment companies give investors diversification. Diversification means spreading money across many investments.\n\nThe most common type of investment company is a mutual fund. Investors pick the fund that best matches their own investment goals, called objectives.\n\nInvestment companies must register with the SEC under the Investment Company Act of 1940. The act defines 3 types of investment companies:\n• Face-amount certificate companies\n• Management companies\n• Unit investment trusts (UITs)\n\nThe act also sets three minimum requirements:\n• Net assets of at least $100,000\n• At least 100 shareholders\n• More than 40% of total assets invested in securities\n\nFace-amount certificates are an old product that is no longer really used. For the test, just know they are a type of investment company.",
